bioinformatics for genomic medicine is a  new initiative  in africa and an opportunity to learn  about the future of medicine  and see how big data computational  biology  data analysis curation and  interpretation are all  key to improved health and a solution  for the growing needs of patients and  healthcare systems around the world  this program will support a growing need  for data analysis skills  amongst biomedical researchers  university students  and industry professionals the upcoming  onyx logic training program on  bioinformatics for genomic medicine  is where you can learn about the role of  big data in basic and translational  biomedical research  we will do so using examples from  chronic and acute infectious diseases  we will see the increasing significance  of novel high throughput data  technologies like next generation  sequencing  and the application of omics data  analysis to diagnostics and treatment  selection  today i'd like to introduce you to this  online training that our team  will be launching this month in  collaboration with medlab convo  and graduates of our online  international programs  that deeply care about the need for  quality bioinformatics education  across africa together with our partners  we designed this program for biologists  clinicians and researchers or students  that are interested in advancing their  understanding of big data in life  science research  this introductory program will not  require  many of the typical prerequisites that  include  computational biology training no need  for  coding proficiency or expertise in  bioinformatics and biostatistics  in fact anyone with a basic familiarity  with cell and molecular biology and an  interest in the future of genomic  medicine  will be able to learn about and apply  big data analytics tools to relevant  data sets  as a result of the short focused and  effective program  we will combine online sessions  self-guided study materials  and practical assignments for an  immersive experience  that has proven to be effective and are  well known and respected  on mclogic academic programs moreover  the support and coordination team will  include some of the brightest and most  energetic partners  i have had a chance to work with  emmanuel anya awa  team lead at metlab convo nigeria  adekunle a de la hoya  faculty at leeds city university and our  own mohit mizunder  so what is the future of medicine with  increasing availability of data  expansion and standardization of high  throughput sequencing  medicine of the future is both more  personalized and precise  driven by data and an integrated  analytics solution  let's take a look at some of these  trends that are leading to a disruption  across all aspects of the traditional  healthcare delivery system  with increased reliance on data and  lower costs of generating detailed and  large data sets  at each clinical interaction we are  faced with an explosion  in data availability however a growing  informational lag  complicates the translation of this data  to practical  and impactful insights at the point of  care this is what we call the analysis  gap  the difference between our ability to  generate new data  and the ability to analyze and interpret  it for the benefit of the individual  patients  as a result there is a disconnect  between lab technicians  researchers and clinicians that form the  biomedical research ecosystem  this disconnect breaks up the cycle of  innovation  due to the lack of data-driven research  skills among otherwise effective  biomedical personnel  that is dealing with increasing demands  in a changing  unpredictable landscape we can see this  gap clearly in this current pandemic  that is affecting every continent  and keeping clinicians epidemiologists  pharmaceutical companies  and even whole governments puzzled the  rate  quality and scale of generated data in  this pandemic has been unprecedented  billions of dollars have been spent to  characterize the virus  and make this data available to the  greater research community  if used properly it can lead to the  development of vaccines  drugs and better diagnostic tests and  yet  over 20 million people have been  infected with thousands of lethal  outcomes  and there's still no clarity about the  future can students and researchers  around the world be empowered to do such  analysis themselves  and share their insights at the local  regional national  and international level this pandemic  has already disrupted health care  but many other more subtle trends and  chronic disease management  rising costs of drug development and  increased fear of communicable diseases  that continue to spread in a changing  climate are exposing the weakness  of this biomedical ecosystem that have  traditionally been driven by  large-scale research projects today the  decentralized data-driven  science is key to the future of clinical  as well as biotechnological innovation  to close this gap we have to focus on  the central issues  that define the future of medicine  people  that work in this field by offering  training support  and effective tools to manage process  analyze and  integrate desperate types of healthcare  data  we can make a lasting impact and change  the future of medicine  that is why this program is built to  provide students of all backgrounds with  a clear perspective on big data  analytics  machine learning and artificial  intelligence and application  to onyx data and the research clinical  and industry applications  our omixlogix step-by-step process is  enabled  by access to complex tools via a simple  and intuitive interface  at the same time exposing participants  to analysis logic  in the context of real data sets and  extrapolation of this logic  to a variety of projects and data sets  with assistance from mentors  and technical support the program is  designed to highlight the role  of big omics data that is shaping modern  biomedical innovation where molecular  data is used for precision diagnostics  big data integration  machine learning and transforming  personalization of treatment selection  and innovative algorithms allow for  unprecedented capabilities  with drug discovery design and  repurposing  the various types of such omics data  describe  in great detail intracellular and  intercellular processes  associated with common diseases these  datasets can be analyzed to understand  the biology behind  host factors and microbiome composition  in our bodies  as well as the environments that we live  in to drive infection and cause  deterioration of health and the  destabilization of the immune system  that can lead to clinical manifestations  of disease  to study such data each type has to flow  through a series of steps  combining into bioinformatics pipelines  that have to be applied with  understanding such analysis  structures data and can be further  studied to understand  how genomic elements are associated with  clinical phenotypes  clinical information derived from such  analysis can include  risk stratification for patients  treatment selection  as well as innovation in target  therapies that are poised to make a huge  difference in how  complex diseases can be treated with  minimal side effects and maximum  efficacy we will use in this program  a case study approach and provide  examples of publicly available data sets  to learn about these topics in each  example we will use a condition  experiment or even an epidemic outbreak  to study  available data and learn about genomic  data analysis  and clinical phenotypes rate of  infection  disease spread or the integration of  data to predict  best treatment options we will look at  into available genomic data  and how it can help us study diseases  like covet 19  a single strand rna virus from the  coronation family  analyzing such genomic data from  publicly accessible repositories  we will learn about viral detection and  the origin  structure function and pathogenesis of  this new pathogen  this includes accurate detection and  characterization of cancer-driving  infections like the epstein-barr virus  human papilloma virus or hepatitis c and  b  which cause over a million of new cancer  cases  every year these kinds of technologies  and the associated need  and bioinformatics skills has already  led  to many important applications in  clinical and pharmaceutical industries  where such skills are in high demand  bioinformaticians  can help extract biologically meaningful  information from data that is often  complex and requires many analysis steps  to explore patterns develop questions  that can be asked from data  ask these questions using analytical  methods and  find answers that can be validated so  that's why we're inviting you  to join this program it will be held  online with one hour online sessions  as well as self-paced materials that are  available through our project-based  educational portal  these three sessions will include  next-generation sequencing big data in  life sciences  how can bioinformatics help us diagnose  and treat infectious diseases  and genomic medicine in and post the era  of covet 19. on september 14th  we will speak about the history of omics  data and its significance  review the entire course and explore the  tools and resources that the program  will offer  on september 15th we will talk about how  bioinformatics can help us diagnose and  treat infectious diseases  where we'll discuss precision medicine  in the era of  viral diseases on september 18th  we will discuss genomic medicine in and  post code 19  where we'll discuss novel coronavirus  genes proteins and the virology of the  disease  and how the data and the various  analysis tools can help in the detection  comparison and diagnosis of different  viral strains  and the identification of the impact of  mutations on the functionality of viral  proteins  to join this program you have to  complete the registration and start  updating your profile  on the educational portal once you have  done that you can start with free  courses  and look into the online courses that  will be offered  for the duration of the program our  previous participants have benefited  from this kind of training  applying what they have learned to their  areas of expertise  including a number of participants that  are currently involved  in biomedical research and even clinical  applications of such  insights in their field once you  complete the registration  you will also receive an account on the  t bioinfo  big data analytical platform for  bioinformatics  big data analysis the program is now  open for registration  after you complete the program you will  receive a certificate of participation  or a certificate of excellence if you  also include  an independent project in your  submission  bioinformatics for genomic medicine is  an opportunity to learn about the future  of medicine and see how big data  computational biology data analysis  curation  and interpretation are all key to  improve healthcare  and a solution for the growing needs of  patients and healthcare systems around  the world  we hope that you join us
 

